blocks.bodyMarkdown:
Contradiction is the developed truth of opposition. Each opposed determination contains the other within its own identity and excludes it at the same time. The contradiction is not simply that a speaker has uttered P and not-P without qualification. It is a structure in which a determination’s self-relation requires a relation that undermines its claim to independence.

Hegel calls contradiction a principle of self-movement because a contradictory determination cannot remain at rest. Its opposed moments both belong to it, yet their form of independent self-subsistence is untenable. The contradiction resolves into a ground or a more concrete unity.

This claim must not be vulgarised. A contradiction is not established by finding any tension, disagreement, or change. One must show:

- the precise determination under examination;
- the opposed moments generated within it;
- how each presupposes the other;
- how their forms of independence conflict;
- what determinate result follows.

Without this reconstruction, “contradiction” becomes a decorative label.

The Marx-Engels-Gesamtausgabe makes clear that *Capital* is not one unproblematic finished text. It includes multiple editions of Volume I, manuscripts for Volumes II and III, preparatory drafts, excerpts, correspondence, and editorial layers. Engels's published Volumes II and III are major scholarly and political achievements, but they are edited constructions from incomplete manuscripts.

A responsible course distinguishes:

- Marx's published texts;
- drafts and notebooks;
- Engels's editions;
- later editorial reconstructions;
- translations with differing terminology.

Textual plurality does not make interpretation impossible. It makes source claims accountable.

